[]byte("<?xml version=\x221.0\x22 encoding=\x22iso-8859-1\x22?>\x0a<!DOCTYPE html PUBLIC \x22-//W3C//DTD XHTML 1.0 Strict//EN\x22 \x22http://www.w3.org/TR/xhtml1/DTD/xhtml1-strict.dtd\x22>\x0a<html xmlns=\x22http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\x22 xml:lang=\x22en\x22>\x0a<head><title>Portable Network Graphics</title>\x0a<meta name=\x22keywords\x22 content=\x22W3C, PNG, portable network graphics, lossless, truecolor, indexed color, transparency, alpha, sRGB, medical imaging, graphic arts, image, graphics, image formats, web graphics, convert to PNG, burn all gifs\x22/>\x0a<style type=\x22text/css\x22><!--\x0abody {background: white; color: black; font-family: Georgia, Times, serif;}\x0adiv.main { margin-left: 5%; margin-right: 2% }\x0ah1, h2, h3 {font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif }\x0ah2 {margin-left:-2%}\x0a.date {color: #000;background: #DFD; font-style: italic; font-weight: bold; \x0avertical-align: top; border: none}\x0a.newslist {margin: 3px 8px}\x0a.banner img { border: none}\x0a--></style>\x0a</head>\x0a<body>\x0a<p class=\x22banner\x22><a href=\x22/\x22><img  alt=\x22W3C \x22 src=\x22/Icons/WWW/w3c_home\x22  style=\x22border: 0\x22/></a>\x0a<a href=\x22https://www.w3.org/Interaction/\x22><img src=\x22https://www.w3.org/Icons/interaction.png\x22 style=\x22border: 0\x22 width=\x22212\x22 height=\x2248\x22 alt=\x22Interaction Domain\x22 /></a>\x0a<a href=\x22/Graphics/Activity\x22><img  alt=\x22Graphics Activity\x22 \x0asrc=\x22/Icons/WWW/graphics_48x48.png\x22/></a>\x0a<img  alt=\x22PNG\x22 src=\x22png_48.png\x22/></p>\x0a\x0a<h1>PNG (Portable Network Graphics)</h1>\x0a\x0a<div class=\x22main\x22>\x0a<h2>What is it</h2>\x0a\x0a<p>PNG is an extensible file format for the lossless, portable,\x0awell-compressed storage of raster images. PNG provides a patent-free\x0areplacement for GIF and can also replace many common uses of\x0aTIFF. Indexed-color, grayscale, and truecolor images are supported,\x0aplus an optional alpha channel for transparency. Sample depths range \x0afrom 1 to 16 bits per component (up to 48bit images for RGB, or 64bit for RGBA). </p>\x0a\x0a<h2>Status</h2>\x0a\x0a<p>The <a href=\x22https://www.w3.org/TR/PNG/\x22>PNG specification</a> was first issued as a <em>W3C Recommendation</em>\x0aon 1<sup>st</sup> October, 1996 (<a\x0ahref=\x22/Press/PNG-PR.en.html\x22>press release</a>) and updated to a second edition incorporating all errata on 10 November 2003.\x0aThis edition is also an ISO standard, ISO/IEC 15948:2003 (E).</p>\x0a\x0a<p>This means it is a mature document that is considered to contribute\x0atowards realising the full potential of the Web. Viewers for PNG are \x0aavailable on many platforms; there are an increasing number of content \x0acreation tools available; and thus \x0a<a href=\x22http://www.libpng.org/pub/png/pngapbr.html\x22>modern browsers</a>\x0a  implement support for it also.</p>\x0a\x0a  <h2>MIME type</h2>\x0a\x0a<p>The MIME type for PNG, <a href=\x22http://www.iana.org/assignments/media-types/image/png\x22>approved</a> on 14 October 1996, is</p>\x0a<pre>image/png</pre>\x0a\x0a<h2>More information</h2>\x0a\x0a<p>The official <a href=\x22http://www.libpng.org/pub/png/\x22>PNG home page</a>\x0ais maintained by Greg Roelofs and includes a frequently updated listing of\x0a<a href=\x22http://www.libpng.org/pub/png/pngapbr.html\x22>Web browsers</a> that \x0asupport PNG (including plug-ins), \x0a<a href=\x22http://www.libpng.org/pub/png/pngapvw.html\x22>helper applications</a>,\x0aand content creation tools (both\x0a<a href=\x22http://www.libpng.org/pub/png/pngaped.html\x22>interactive editors</a>\x0aand <a href=\x22http://www.libpng.org/pub/png/pngapcv.html\x22>conversion tools</a>).</p>\x0a\x0a<p>You can also read \x0a<a href=\x22http://www.libpng.org/pub/png/slashpng-1999.html\x22>The Story of PNG</a></p>\x0a\x0a<!-- need to add description of alpha and antialiasing once written -->\x0a\x0a<p>If you are not sure just what the <em>gamma</em> and\x0a<em>chromaticity</em> features actually mean in real life, look at\x0athis <a href=\x22platform.html\x22>description</a> to see how they\x0acontribute towards improved cross-platform graphics. PNG also has a\x0anovel <a href =\x0a\x22http://www.libpng.org/pub/png/pngpics.html\x22><em>interlacing\x0ascheme</em></a> which provides a usable graphic faster; and the\x0ainclusion of <em>metadata</em> in the file means that search engines\x0acan find graphics based on their descriptions rather than their\x0afilenames. </p>\x0a\x0a<!-- seems to have gone away \x0a\x0a<p></p>A sample implementation of this, based on the Harvest\x0asystem, is <a href=\x0a\x22http://www.hensa.ac.uk/Harvest/brokers/png/query.html\x22>available for\x0atesting</a> (on a very small dataset; try searching for <em>town</em>\x0ato get the idea of what will be possible in the future).</p>\x0a-->\x0a\x0a<h2>Browser testing</h2>\x0a\x0a<p>See if your <a href=\x22Inline-img.html\x22>browser supports PNG</a> and\x0aif so, whether it handles <a href=\x22all_seven.html\x22>gamma</a> and <a\x0ahref=\x22inline-alpha.html\x22>alpha</a> correctly.</p>\x0a\x0a<!--\x0a<p> As long ago as 14 Feb 1995, <A\x0ahref=\x22http://www.compuserve.com/\x22>CompuServe</a> announced <A\x0ahref=\x22CS-950214.html\x22>support for PNG</a>.  They will also fund and\x0apublish an intellectual property review to ensure that this emerging\x0astandard is free of patent infringements.</p>\x0a-->\x0a\x0a</div>\x0a<hr class=\x22xhtml\x22/>\x0a\x0a\x0a<address><a href=\x22/people/chris\x22>Chris</a><br class=\x22xhtml\x22/>\x0aCreated: <tt>14 Feb 1995</tt><br class=\x22xhtml\x22/>\x0aLast updated: <tt>$Date: 2006/03/17 16:22:43 $</tt><br class=\x22xhtml\x22/>\x0a\x0a<a href=\x22http://validator.w3.org/\x22><img style=\x22border:0;width:88px;height:31px\x22 src=\x22https://www.w3.org/Icons/valid-xhtml10\x22 alt=\x22Valid XHTML 1.0!\x22 height=\x2231\x22 width=\x2288\x22 /></a>\x0a\x0a<a href=\x22http://jigsaw.w3.org/css-validator\x22><img style=\x22border:0;width:88px;height:3